# interactor-trailing-blank-on-ccl — three interactor tests fail on CCL

STATUS: DONE in 2.0.11. Found 2026-08-28 while checking the compiler
branch on the CCL lane; fixed the same day, together with two further
CCL failures it had been hiding.

## Symptom

`make test-ccl` fails three tests in INTERACTOR-SUITE, all with the same
shape: one extra `:BLANK` at the end of the input.

    MULTI-LINE-FORMS-READ-ACROSS-LINES
      ((+ 1 2) :BLANK) can't be destructured against (FORM):
      it contains 2 elements, and exactly 1 are expected.

    COMMA-COMMAND-READ-TAKES-SEXPS-EXCEPT-COMMA-LINES
      ((SETQ X 1) :BLANK #S(INPUT-COMMAND …)) against (SEXP CMD)

    COMMAND-READ-TAKES-LINES-AS-COMMANDS-EXCEPT-FORMS
      (#S(INPUT-COMMAND …) #S(INPUT-COMMAND …) (PRINT X) :BLANK :BLANK)
      against (CMD MOTION FORM BLANK)

`make test-sbcl` is green on all three. The whole rest of the corpus is
green on both.

## Repro

    make test-ccl

Or, smaller — the first failing test is:

    (read-all #'command-read (format nil "(+ 1~%   2)~%"))
    SBCL => ((+ 1 2))
    CCL  => ((+ 1 2) :BLANK)

## Where it comes from

All three failures go through the same path: a line is read, recognised
as the start of a Lisp form, pushed back with

    unread-line-from-input-context   (autolisp-interactor/source/input.lisp:24)

which replaces the context's stream by a CONCATENATED-STREAM of
[the line] + [a newline] + [the rest], and the form is then READ from

that stream.

What differs between the hosts is what is left in the stream after READ
returns, and therefore whether the NEXT `read-line` yields an empty line
(`:BLANK`) or `:EOF`. CCL leaves a trailing empty line where SBCL does
not.

That is a host difference in stream/READ behaviour, not a bug in either
implementation: the standard does not say whether READ consumes the
whitespace that terminated the object. The interactor's line reader is
simply written to one host's answer.

## Not from this branch

  - The compiler branch (2.0.1 .. 2.0.10) touches neither
    `autolisp-interactor/` nor `autolisp-reader/`:
    `git diff --stat <merge-base>..HEAD -- clautolisp/interactor
    clautolisp/autolisp-reader` is empty.
  - `autolisp-interactor/source/input.lisp` and its tests were last
    touched at 1.8.37, long before 2.0.
  - Nothing in the failure involves AutoLISP evaluation; it is Common
    Lisp READ on a string stream.

So this is longstanding, and the question worth answering is why the CCL
CI lane did not already show it — a different CCL version, or the lane
not actually running this suite. That is the first thing to check.

## Suggested fix

Do not depend on where READ left the stream. Two candidates:

  - After reading a form through an unread line, consume the rest of the
    current line explicitly (read to end-of-line and discard it if it is
    whitespace only). Deliberate rather than incidental, and identical on
    every host.
  - Or have the sexp path use a stream position it controls, instead of
    a CONCATENATED-STREAM whose EOF behaviour is what varies.

Either way the test to keep is the one that already caught it: reading a
multi-line form from a script ending in a newline must yield exactly the
forms, on every host.

## Why it matters now

GitLab CI is the source of truth and the CCL suite is one of its lanes,
so a red CCL lane blocks merging the compiler branch to master even
though the compiler is green on both hosts.

PJB:
clhs read indicates:

    read parses the printed representation of an object from
    input-stream and builds such an object. 

    read-preserving-whitespace is like read but preserves any
    whitespace[2] character that delimits the printed representation
    of the object. read-preserving-whitespace is exactly like read
    when the recursive-p argument to read-preserving-whitespace is
    true. 

    When *read-suppress* is false, read throws away the delimiting
    character required by certain printed representations if it is a
    whitespace[2] character; but read preserves the character (using
    unread-char) if it is syntactically meaningful, because it could
    be the start of the next expression. 

Perhaps the setting of *read-suppress* matters in this case?  Note
that if CL:READ uses CL:UNREAD-CHAR, we cannot use CL:UNREAD-CHAR
ourselves, because CL:UNREAD-CHAR has space in the buffer only for one
character. (we may wrap a string-input-stream with the input stream in
a concatenated-stream instead).


## RESOLVED — 2.0.11

pjb's CLHS quote above is the whole diagnosis. READ discards its
delimiting character only when that character is WHITESPACE; the
delimiter of `(+ 1 2)` is the closing paren, which is not, so the newline
after it stays in the stream. Whether it then survives a
CONCATENATED-STREAM boundary is where the hosts part company. Measured
directly, on the exact shape the interactor builds:

    line "(+ 1", rest "   2)\n"
      SBCL  next read-line => :EOF
      CCL   next read-line => ""

Neither is wrong. So the fix is not to pick a host but to stop asking the
question.

READ-SEXP-FROM-INPUT-CONTEXT now accumulates LINES and parses with
READ-FROM-STRING, which RETURNS the position it stopped at — what remains
is a fact rather than an inference. Whatever follows the form on its last
line is pushed back, so `(print x) (print y)` on one line still reads as
two forms, and nothing manufactures an empty line the user never typed.

pjb's other warning is why the push-back is a CONCATENATED-STREAM and not
UNREAD-CHAR: the standard guarantees only ONE character of push-back, and
READ may already have spent it (`preserves the character (using
unread-char) if it is syntactically meaningful'). That is noted at
PUSH-BACK-STRING-INTO-INPUT-CONTEXT so it is not undone later.

*READ-SUPPRESS* turned out not to be involved: it is NIL throughout, and
the divergence reproduces with it untouched.

## WHAT THIS WAS HIDING — the part that matters more

CCL aborts at the FIRST failing suite. With the interactor suite failing,
the CCL lane never reached the other 18 suites at all. Fixing it revealed
two further failures, and both were real:

  1. A BROKEN DOCSTRING IN 2.0.9, mine. CALL-IN-FILE-COMPILATION-UNIT's
     docstring contained an unescaped double quote, so it ended early and
     left `SPEED 3 compiles the whole file at once' as stray forms in the
     function body. SBCL DELETES the dead variable read; CCL evaluates it
     and signals `Unbound variable: SPEED'. Every LOAD failed on CCL.
     Both behaviours are permitted, which is exactly why one host cannot
     certify a change.
     A scan of every .lisp file in the tree for the same defect —
     docstring closing mid-prose — found this one and no others.

  2. THE ARTEFACT WRITER WAS SBCL-SHAPED, twice.
     - CCL REFUSES a COMPILE-FILE destination whose type is not its own
       ("Compile destination ... is not a lx64fsl file!"), so asking for a
       .lap directly worked on SBCL and failed on CCL. This is pjb's
       .lx86fas remark arriving as a hard constraint: a host FASL type is
       the host's business. COMPILE-FILE now names its own output and the
       result is MOVED to the .lap — which is what "rename the fasl" meant
       all along.
     - CL:RENAME-FILE replaces an existing target on SBCL and signals
       "File exists" on CCL, so recompiling an application succeeded on
       one host and failed on the other. UIOP:RENAME-FILE-OVERWRITING-
       TARGET is the portable form.
     Copy-then-rename rather than a straight rename, too: the host writes
     its FASL beside the generated source in the temporary directory,
     frequently a different filesystem from the artefact's destination.

## The lesson recorded

The CCL lane had not been run once across ten slices of compiler work.
Every one of those slices was reported green on the strength of SBCL
alone. Two of the three bugs above were introduced by that work and were
invisible on SBCL by construction — a dead-code deletion and a
default-output-type. Run both hosts before calling a slice done.
